#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 780.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069604} :activity/id #uuid "1e92c4a4-e0ad-5ee8-8851-82d431bca7f7" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049309 :topic/id #uuid "eb56b359-bf3c-5bc4-a26b-629e05c1d464" :topic/math-academy-id 451} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069604 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186070026 :tutorial/content "**Absolute value inequalities** combine two basic concepts: absolute value and inequality. The absolute value of a number is its distance from zero, while an inequality specifies a range of values.\n\nConsider a simple absolute value inequality, as follows:\n\n$$\n|x| < 7\n$$\n\nThis inequality specifies every number $x$ that is less than a distance of $7$ from $0$. So, it represents the same thing as the following compound inequality:\n\n$$\n-7 < x < 7\n$$\n\nTo solve an absolute value inequality, we first convert it to a compound inequality to remove the absolute value. Then, we can solve the compound inequality using the usual methods.\n\nTo illustrate, let's solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n|2x+3| \\leq 5\n$$\n\nFirst, we interpret the inequality: the inequality specifies every number $2x+3$ whose distance from zero is less than or equal to $5.$ So, it represents the same thing as the following compound inequality:\n\n$$\n-5 \\leq 2x+3 \\leq 5\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve the compound inequality using the usual methods:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5\\le 2x+3\\le 5 \\\\ -8\\le 2x\\le 2 \\\\ -4\\le x\\le 1\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "53ecd5cc-486a-584d-abc8-f75ec26f12cc" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8825 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "e46e2f60-2005-54d4-8f5c-97b81542dd48" :step/math-academy-id 145541 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069605}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069605 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068717 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068491 :question/id #uuid "60c2e025-0b7e-5ac2-afec-adeb5f65eed1" :question/math-academy-id "e-14222"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e107f878-5b22-54bf-90fc-b8ea8a22c77d" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048267} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050734}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069130} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069131} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069289} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511567} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511580}] :knowledge-point/title "Absolute Value Inequalities Containing a Less Than (or Equal To) Symbol"} :step/id #uuid "461b27d5-3e08-5e71-9e57-464e06a7b559" :step/math-academy-id 145542 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069606}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069606 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068718 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068510 :question/id #uuid "12d8aea9-62dd-5181-bd6e-54c11eba4152" :question/math-academy-id "e-14708"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "65964b91-8f0e-5ffc-97d3-e5b87a6bade5" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069132} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069133} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069294} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511521}] :knowledge-point/title "Cases Where the Coefficient in the Absolute Value Is Negative"} :step/id #uuid "95df2774-fa6c-5c44-85c3-1203c060ec1a" :step/math-academy-id 145546 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069607}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069607 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186070027 :tutorial/content "We take a similar approach for absolute value inequalities involving the \"greater than\" symbol. To illustrate, consider the following absolute value inequality:\n\n$$\n|x| \\geq 6\n$$\n\nTo solve this absolute value inequality, we convert it into a compound inequality to remove the absolute value. The inequality specifies every number $x$ whose distance from zero is greater than or equal to $6.$ This corresponds to the following compound inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & \\le -6 & \\text{or} &  & x & \\ge 6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use the word \"or\" instead of \"and\" since only one of the inequalities $x\\leq-6$ or $x\\geq 6$ needs to be true for $|x| \\geq 6$ to be true." :tutorial/id #uuid "93e8452a-53ea-5e58-807a-00e95af99af1" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8830 :tutorial/title "Greater Than (or Equal To) Absolute Value Inequalities"} :step/id #uuid "3706f0b6-4695-54b2-974b-2b4f77638739" :step/math-academy-id 145550 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069608}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069608 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068719 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068493 :question/id #uuid "8497ea21-c194-5bbf-b501-a0e9749d8dc8" :question/math-academy-id "e-14227"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4f7e042-a8fa-50c8-a502-1397138dc380" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050680}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069134} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069135} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069290} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511463} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511490} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511573}] :knowledge-point/title "Absolute Value Inequalities Containing a Greater Than (or Equal To) Symbol"} :step/id #uuid "419f8ba9-27c0-5918-8ecd-eff659df7bb5" :step/math-academy-id 145551}] :activity/title "Absolute Value Inequalities" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186068978}]}
